{"results":[{"url":"https://kutv.com/news/politics/utah-county-clerk-acknowledges-error-after-thousands-receive-voter-id-notices","final_url":"https://kutv.com/news/politics/utah-county-clerk-acknowledges-error-after-thousands-receive-voter-id-notices","title":"Utah County clerk acknowledges error after thousands receive voter ID notices","description":"The Utah County clerk on acknowledged thousands of voters incorrectly received notices telling them they need to submit a copy of their identification with thei","language":"en","text":"# Utah County clerk acknowledges error after thousands receive voter ID notices\n\n---\n\nby Nate Carlisle, KUTV\n\nFri, October 23, 2026 at 7:29 PM\n\nUpdated Fri, October 23, 2026 at 7:46 PM\n\n6VIEW ALL PHOTOS\n\nThe Utah County clerk on acknowledged thousands of voters incorrectly received notices telling them they need to submit a copy of their identification with their ballot. (KUTV)\n\nPROVO, Utah (KUTV) — The Utah County clerk on acknowledged thousands of voters incorrectly received notices telling them they need to submit a copy of their identification with their ballot.\n\nThe notices were meant to go to a few voters who did not provide government ID at the time they registered, said Aaron Davidson, the county clerk. Instead, they went to a wider swath of new voters and even a few who voted previously.\n\n\"So it was a clerical error,\" Davidson said. \"There were a total of 6,144 first-time voter, first-time voter notifications that went out... I did go through and we did find 71 that they have voted in the past.\"\n\n## MORE | Utah voter registration\n\nUtah County Clerk MailerPDF preview\n\nThe Utah County Clerk acknowledged thousands of voters there incorrectly received notices telling them they need to submit a copy of their identification with their ballot.\n\nView PDF\n\nDavidson attributed the mistakes to miscommunication with the state's driver license database -- many Utahns register to vote when they obtain their license -- and the failure of some of the clerk staff to check a box when people voted in previous elections.\n\nVoters who provided identification during registration or who have voted previously will not be required to resubmit ID.\n\n\"If they send it in or they don't send it in, we're still gonna count the ballot,\" Davidson said.\n\nRegistering to vote was a smooth process for Jennifer Penrod on Friday. She recently moved from the California Bay Area to pursue a master's degree at Brigham Young University.\n\n\"So I just walked into the office,\" Penrod said, \"filled out the voter registration, and with my last four of my social and my passport, and that was what it took.\"\n\nAn employee at the clerk's office told Penrod her ballot will be mailed next week.\n\nYet, Sarah McConkie worries about mistakes like that at the Utah County clerk. McConkie works with the nonpartisan advocacy group Indivisible Utah County.\n\n\"We're concerned that if people are getting in the mail this notice with their ballots saying that you need to take an extra step that you don't need to,\" McConkie said, \"it could dissuade people from voting, make it feel more complicated, intimidating, cumbersome than it needs to be.\"\n\nDavidson said some of the voters who -- rightly or not -- received the notification have already returned their ballots with a copy of their ID.\n\nThe notices went to a single-digit percentage of the Utah County electorate. Davidson said voter discouragement concerns only seem to come from those not impacted.\n\n\"I've never heard that complaint from somebody that it is affected, that got that notification,\" Davidson said.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_","author":"Nate Carlisle, KUTV","published_date":"2026-10-24T01:29:16.000Z","latency_ms":180.14448099984293,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}